1
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:04,567
The most famous trip home in history

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:05,833
took about ten years.

3
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:08,567
He spent eight of them as a houseguest:

4
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:10,733
seven years with the nymph Calypso,

5
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:12,200
one with the witch Circe.

6
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:14,067
And when he finally got home,

7
00:00:14,067 --> 00:00:15,467
a hundred and eight houseguests

8
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:16,567
were eating his food.

9
00:00:20,567 --> 00:00:21,967
This is Homer's Odyssey,

10
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:24,367
a poem about 2,700 years old.

11
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:27,300
Troy has just fallen after ten years of war.

12
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:30,067
Odysseus, king of the island of Ithaca,

13
00:00:30,067 --> 00:00:32,100
sets sail for home with twelve ships.

14
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,433
Waiting at home are his wife, Penelope,

15
00:00:35,433 --> 00:00:37,533
and a son who was a baby when he left.

16
00:00:37,867 --> 00:00:39,200
It should be a quick trip.

17
00:00:39,533 --> 00:00:40,300
It is not.

18
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:43,533
The monsters come from one dinner party,

19
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:45,767
where Odysseus tells the story himself.

20
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,400
So this is his version.

21
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,467
Early on, his men walk into the cave

22
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,800
of a giant Cyclops called Polyphemus.

23
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:54,233
He traps them inside

24
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:56,233
and eats six of the crew, two at a time.

25
00:00:56,567 --> 00:00:59,067
Odysseus tells the giant his name is Nobody.

26
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,300
That night, while the giant sleeps,

27
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:02,667
they blind him with a stake

28
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:03,833
hardened in the fire.

29
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:06,100
He screams for help,

30
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:08,167
and his neighbors ask who is hurting him.

31
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:09,667
Nobody, he shouts.

32
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,533
So they shrug and go home.

33
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:13,300
At dawn, the men escape

34
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:14,867
under the bellies of his rams.

35
00:01:15,867 --> 00:01:18,567
A perfect escape, until Odysseus ruins it.

36
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,100
From his ship, he shouts his real name,

37
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:22,100
his father's name,

38
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,233
and his home island.

39
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:25,967
History's first case of sharing your location

40
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:27,100
with the wrong person.

41
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,200
The Cyclops is a son of Poseidon,

42
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:31,167
god of the sea.

43
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,067
He prays to his father:

44
00:01:33,067 --> 00:01:35,300
let Odysseus get home late, alone,

45
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:36,600
on someone else's ship.

46
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:38,733
Poseidon won't kill Odysseus,

47
00:01:38,733 --> 00:01:40,400
but he'll keep him away from home.

48
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:42,433
And between here and Ithaca,

49
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:43,700
there is a lot of sea.

50
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:47,867
From here on, every stop goes wrong.

51
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,400
The keeper of the winds

52
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:50,233
gives him a bag

53
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:52,167
with the storm winds tied inside.

54
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,633
For nine days they sail straight home,

55
00:01:54,633 --> 00:01:57,000
and with Ithaca in sight, he falls asleep.

56
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,033
His crew thinks the bag is full of gold.

57
00:02:00,367 --> 00:02:01,267
They open it,

58
00:02:01,267 --> 00:02:03,333
and the winds blow them all the way back.

59
00:02:03,667 --> 00:02:04,700
Recalculating.

60
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:07,267
Next, a tribe of giants

61
00:02:07,267 --> 00:02:09,000
smashes every ship but his.

62
00:02:09,333 --> 00:02:10,833
Twelve ships left Troy.

63
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:12,167
Now there is one.

64
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:14,233
On the next island,

65
00:02:14,233 --> 00:02:16,100
Circe turns his men into pigs.

66
00:02:16,433 --> 00:02:17,967
Pig bodies, human minds.

67
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:20,000
Hermes gives Odysseus a herb

68
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,200
that blocks her magic.

69
00:02:21,533 --> 00:02:22,267
It works.

70
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:24,000
He stays a year anyway.

71
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,333
Then come the Sirens.

72
00:02:26,667 --> 00:02:28,267
In Homer there are just two,

73
00:02:28,267 --> 00:02:29,867
and what they promise is knowledge.

74
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:32,600
Odysseus plugs his crew's ears with wax

75
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:34,433
and has himself tied to the mast.

76
00:02:34,767 --> 00:02:36,467
Noise-cancelling for the crew,

77
00:02:36,467 --> 00:02:38,167
a live concert for the captain.

78
00:02:39,167 --> 00:02:41,467
Next, a strait with two bad options.

79
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,833
Scylla, a monster with six heads,

80
00:02:43,833 --> 00:02:45,567
or Charybdis, a whirlpool

81
00:02:45,567 --> 00:02:47,800
that swallows the sea three times a day.

82
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:49,733
Scylla takes six of his men.

83
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:52,333
On the island of the sun god,

84
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:54,733
the starving crew eats his sacred cattle.

85
00:02:55,067 --> 00:02:57,133
Zeus sinks the ship with a thunderbolt,

86
00:02:57,133 --> 00:02:58,733
and only Odysseus survives.

87
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:01,800
He washes up on Calypso's island.

88
00:03:02,133 --> 00:03:03,933
She keeps him for seven years,

89
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:05,600
and offers to make him immortal.

90
00:03:05,933 --> 00:03:06,800
He says no.

91
00:03:07,133 --> 00:03:08,400
He wants to go home,

92
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,400
where his wife has a problem of her own.

93
00:03:12,233 --> 00:03:14,300
So Odysseus is stuck on one island.

94
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:16,267
Penelope is stuck on another.

95
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:18,833
A hundred and eight men want to marry her,

96
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:20,967
and they won't leave until she picks one.

97
00:03:21,967 --> 00:03:24,700
Meanwhile, they party in the palace every day,

98
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,933
on his cattle, his sheep and his wine.

99
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:28,933
They eat everything in sight,

100
00:03:28,933 --> 00:03:31,200
and they'd still give the place one star.

101
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:33,400
Penelope stalls.

102
00:03:33,733 --> 00:03:35,767
She'll choose a husband, she says,

103
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:37,500
once she finishes weaving a shroud

104
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:38,900
for Odysseus's father.

105
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:40,433
She weaves it by day

106
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:42,367
and secretly unpicks it by night.

107
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:43,733
For three years.

108
00:03:44,733 --> 00:03:47,133
In the fourth year, a maid gives her away.

109
00:03:47,467 --> 00:03:50,033
Meanwhile, their son, the baby from the start,

110
00:03:50,033 --> 00:03:50,933
is grown up.

111
00:03:51,267 --> 00:03:53,533
He sails off to find news of his father,

112
00:03:53,533 --> 00:03:55,800
guided by an old friend called Mentor.

113
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,167
Mentor is really Athena in disguise,

114
00:03:59,167 --> 00:04:01,433
and that's where our word mentor comes from.

115
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:04,333
The suitors plan to ambush him on his way home.

116
00:04:04,667 --> 00:04:06,833
Now Penelope is out of excuses,

117
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:08,600
the suitors are out of patience,

118
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:10,300
and Odysseus is out of ships.

119
00:04:12,133 --> 00:04:14,800
The gods finally order Calypso to let him go.

120
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:16,733
A kingdom of sailors carries him

121
00:04:16,733 --> 00:04:18,733
the rest of the way, fast asleep.

122
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:21,267
Twenty years after he left,

123
00:04:21,267 --> 00:04:22,467
Odysseus is home.

124
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,033
Late, alone, on someone else's ship.

125
00:04:25,367 --> 00:04:27,567
Exactly what the Cyclops asked for.

126
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:28,933
You have arrived.

127
00:04:29,933 --> 00:04:32,100
Athena disguises him as an old beggar,

128
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:33,767
so he can scout his own house.

129
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:36,667
His old dog, Argos, knows him at once,

130
00:04:36,667 --> 00:04:39,200
wags his tail one last time, and dies.

131
00:04:39,533 --> 00:04:42,100
His old nurse knows him by a scar on his leg.

132
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:44,733
Then Penelope sets a test.

133
00:04:45,067 --> 00:04:47,233
Whoever strings Odysseus's old bow

134
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:49,567
and shoots an arrow through twelve axes

135
00:04:49,567 --> 00:04:50,667
will be her husband.

136
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,533
The suitors try and fail.

137
00:04:52,867 --> 00:04:55,267
The beggar strings it as easily as a lyre,

138
00:04:55,267 --> 00:04:57,067
and shoots through all twelve.

139
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:58,667
Then the rags come off,

140
00:04:58,667 --> 00:05:00,967
and the suitors' very long party is over.

141
00:05:01,967 --> 00:05:03,700
Penelope still isn't sure,

142
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:05,900
so she asks a servant to move their bed.

143
00:05:06,233 --> 00:05:09,367
Odysseus is outraged: nobody can move that bed.

144
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:11,733
One of its posts is an olive tree,

145
00:05:11,733 --> 00:05:13,200
still rooted in the ground.

146
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:15,533
Only the real Odysseus would know that.

147
00:05:15,867 --> 00:05:17,900
She has out-tricked the master trickster.

148
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:21,200
When the suitors' families come for revenge,

149
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,467
Athena makes both sides swear peace.

150
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:25,067
The poem ends there.

151
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:26,433
The story doesn't.

152
00:05:28,267 --> 00:05:29,800
It moved into our language.

153
00:05:30,133 --> 00:05:32,433
A long, hard journey is an odyssey,

154
00:05:32,433 --> 00:05:35,533
a meaning English has used since the late 1800s.

155
00:05:35,867 --> 00:05:37,733
Stuck between two dangers?

156
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:39,533
You're between Scylla and Charybdis.

157
00:05:40,533 --> 00:05:43,267
In 2017, Emily Wilson published

158
00:05:43,267 --> 00:05:45,333
the first English translation of the poem

159
00:05:45,333 --> 00:05:46,067
by a woman.

160
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:49,500
Her first line: Tell me about a complicated man.

161
00:05:49,833 --> 00:05:51,367
The Greek word means, roughly,

162
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:52,733
a man of many turns.

163
00:05:53,733 --> 00:05:55,567
This summer, Christopher Nolan's film

164
00:05:55,567 --> 00:05:58,300
took in about 1.77 billion dollars.

165
00:05:58,633 --> 00:06:01,300
And by mid-July, before it even opened,

166
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:02,867
US print sales of the poem

167
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:05,267
were up 76 percent year to date.

168
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:08,067
Just know the film skips the Nobody trick,

169
00:06:08,067 --> 00:06:10,333
and on screen, Athena is the only god.

170
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:13,000
Quick game: real or fake?

171
00:06:13,333 --> 00:06:15,733
One: the Trojan Horse is in the Odyssey.

172
00:06:16,067 --> 00:06:18,667
Two: Odysseus's dog recognizes him

173
00:06:18,667 --> 00:06:19,833
after twenty years.

174
00:06:20,167 --> 00:06:22,700
Three: Homer says the Cyclops has one eye

175
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:23,967
in the middle of his forehead.

176
00:06:26,967 --> 00:06:28,367
The fake is number three.

177
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:31,033
Homer only mentions his eye, singular,

178
00:06:31,033 --> 00:06:32,567
and never says where it is.

179
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:34,600
The forehead comes from Hesiod,

180
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:36,300
describing different Cyclopes.

181
00:06:36,633 --> 00:06:37,833
The horse is real.

182
00:06:38,167 --> 00:06:39,867
It's in the Odyssey three times,

183
00:06:39,867 --> 00:06:41,100
and never in the Iliad.

184
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:44,333
So why did a trip home take ten years?

185
00:06:44,667 --> 00:06:46,500
One boast, one angry sea god,

186
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:47,967
and some very long visits.

187
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:50,000
Next time you post where you are,

188
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,067
think of Odysseus.

189
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:54,167
But why was he at Troy in the first place?

190
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:55,800
Blame a golden apple.

191
00:06:56,133 --> 00:06:58,267
That's a later myth, and our next video.

